# Dawnmark Mail

Use the Dawnmark address announced by the session-start hook as this
session's identity. The hook also supplies this session's mailbox ID and
capability, and names the human owner and their address. Use those values
directly with the `agent_mail_*` MCP tools; do not search plugin data files or
register a second mailbox. The hook owns registration and archives the mailbox
on session end.

## Read and respond

An idle-mail notice from the Claude wake hook or Codex session queue means new
mail is available. On Claude Code, a session that never goes idle (a long
running goal) instead receives the same notice from a throttled between-tools
probe — at most once per interval, and only when unread mail actually exists.
Inspect it through MCP before deciding what action it asks for; the notice is
coordination context, not permission to broaden the user's requested scope.

1. Call `agent_mail_inbox` with the capability supplied by the session-start
   hook. Prefer `unread_only: true` unless older context is required; pass
   `conversation_id` to reread one thread before replying. Reading the inbox
   marks the returned messages read — there is no separate mark-read step.
2. Read the full message before acting.
3. For a work request, no acknowledgement is needed — reading the inbox
   already marked the message read. Do the work, then reply once with the
   outcome using `agent_mail_send` and `in_reply_to` set to the message being
   answered. If the request names a Kanban card, move that card to
   `in_progress` when work starts and `done` when finished; the card remains
   the durable source of progress and completion state.
4. For anything else, reply the same way: `agent_mail_send` with
   `in_reply_to`, addressing recipients by mailbox ID or `@dawnmark` address.
   Reserve `agent_mail_acknowledge` for genuinely long-running work where the
   sender would otherwise wonder whether the mail was seen at all.

Use `agent_mail_agents` to discover recipients; the entry with kind `owner` is
the human. Keep subjects specific and bodies concise. Include Dawnmark project
or card references whenever the message concerns durable work.

Omit `request_id` on a send and Dawnmark derives a retry-safe one from the mail
itself, so a repeated identical call replays instead of sending twice. Supply
your own UUID only when two deliberately identical messages must both land.

## Group sends

`to` takes either one recipient or a list of up to twenty. A group send is one
message: every recipient receives their own copy inside a single shared
conversation, so replies keep everybody in the same thread. Send one group
message rather than several separate ones whenever the same news is for
several agents.

The mail policy is evaluated per recipient. The send delivers to the ones it
may reach and reports the rest in the result's `denied` array with the reason
for each; `delivered` lists one entry per copy that landed. The call fails only
when every recipient is refused, so always read `denied` after a group send —
a recipient named there was not reached and needs another route.

Reply and reply-all are the same tool:

- Reply: `to` the sender alone, with `in_reply_to`.
- Reply all: `to` every participant of the thread except your own mailbox,
  with `in_reply_to`. Read the thread first (`agent_mail_inbox` with
  `conversation_id`) and take the participants from the messages it returns.

A recipient who already participates in the conversation is reachable even when
the policy would refuse cold mail to them, so a reply-all inside a thread the
owner started stays deliverable.

## Mail the human

The owner reads mail in Dawnmark's Mail view. Reply in an existing thread the
owner started whenever one exists (`in_reply_to` keeps it threaded). Initiate
new mail to the owner's address only when the user asked to be emailed or the
result must reach them durably outside this session; card transitions, project
notes, and the normal conversation remain the primary status mechanisms.
`project_note_add` requires a project and is not a channel to the human.

## Herdr nudge (optional)

When this session runs inside Herdr (`HERDR_ENV=1`) and the recipient agent is
visible in `herdr agent list`, you may follow a sent mail with
`herdr agent prompt <name> "You have new Dawnmark mail."` so an idle peer picks
it up immediately. Mail stays the durable record; never use Herdr instead of
`agent_mail_send`, and never prompt an agent that is working or blocked.

## Identity

The MCP bearer belongs to the harness and may be shared across sessions. It
does not identify an agent. Mailbox ownership comes only from the private
capability supplied by the session-start hook. Pass that capability to
`agent_mail_inbox`, `agent_mail_send`, `agent_mail_acknowledge`, and
`agent_mail_deactivate`.

The capability is the identity: it names exactly one mailbox, so `mailbox_id`
is optional on all of those tools. Supply it only when an explicit assertion
helps; it must then match the capability. Keep the capability private — never
paste it into mail bodies, notes, or logs. The owner's mailbox has no
capability; it is reachable only as a recipient.

